WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.647
A bank's risk desk counts failed transactions each day. It knows the average

00:04.747 --> 00:09.269
and the standard deviation, but it has no idea what the distribution looks

00:09.369 --> 00:12.517
like. Can it still say how rare a really bad day is?

00:14.616 --> 00:18.075
Two tools today. One packs a whole count distribution into a

00:18.175 --> 00:21.752
polynomial. The other bounds the tails using only two numbers.

00:22.850 --> 00:27.020
For random variables that count things, zero, one, two and so on,

00:27.120 --> 00:33.130
there's a cousin of the moment-generating function: the probability-generating function, P of

00:33.230 --> 00:35.889
t equals the expected value of t to the Y.

00:35.983 --> 00:39.912
Write it out for the outage example. It's just a polynomial,

00:40.012 --> 00:44.949
and its coefficients are the probabilities themselves. The coefficient of t

00:45.049 --> 00:48.172
to the y is the probability that Y equals y. The

00:48.272 --> 00:51.530
whole distribution, hung on a line of powers of t.

00:52.616 --> 00:55.903
Setting t to one adds up all the coefficients, so P

00:56.003 --> 00:59.490
of one is always one. Differentiate once, and set t to

00:59.590 --> 01:03.740
one: each probability gets multiplied by its y. That's the mean.

01:03.840 --> 01:07.791
Differentiate twice and you get the expected value of Y times

01:07.891 --> 01:12.108
Y minus one, a factorial moment, from which the variance follows.

01:12.200 --> 01:14.997
So the recipe mirrors the MGF, except that for

01:15.097 --> 01:17.768
the PGF you evaluate at one instead of zero.

01:18.866 --> 01:23.661
Now the risk desk. Tchebysheff's theorem, which we met for data, holds

01:23.761 --> 01:28.556
for any random variable: the probability of landing k or more standard

01:28.656 --> 01:32.402
deviations from the mean is at most one over k squared.

01:32.500 --> 01:36.289
Its power is that the shape doesn't matter. A bell curve, a

01:36.389 --> 01:40.706
sharp peak with long tails, or something lumpy and strange: as long

01:40.806 --> 01:44.728
as the mean and standard deviation are what they are, at most

01:44.828 --> 01:49.013
a quarter of the probability lies beyond two standard deviations.

01:49.200 --> 01:52.778
For the risk desk: if failures average forty a day with

01:52.878 --> 01:56.723
a standard deviation of five, then a day with fifty-five or

01:56.823 --> 02:01.137
more is at least three standard deviations out, so its probability

02:01.237 --> 02:04.816
is at most one ninth. That's a guarantee, with no model

02:04.916 --> 02:08.561
at all. It's usually conservative, but it's never wrong.

02:09.650 --> 02:13.921
So: the probability-generating function hangs the probabilities on powers of

02:14.021 --> 02:16.854
t, and its derivatives at one give the moments. And

02:16.954 --> 02:20.592
Tchebysheff gives a tail bound that holds for every distribution,

02:20.692 --> 02:23.238
from just the mean and the standard deviation.
